From 73af61efe6a09e2f4be1ce5ffb9da652292f7690 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:20:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(deepseek-v4.1): split the indexer's rows across ranks in
 SP prefill

Sequence-parallel prefill gathers every row before attention, and the
indexer is replicated: each TP rank projected, scored and selected the same
top-k positions for every row of the chunk. Scoring grows with context
depth. On one 4,096-token chunk at TP 3 it took 36 ms at 8K of context and
342 ms at 200K, 25% of the chunk and all of its growth with depth.

Under SP each rank now builds index queries and selects positions only for
its own rows (SPRows.start to start + real_rows), then all-gathers the
selected positions into the shared top-k buffer before sparse MLA. Rows are
independent. The BF16 head-weight projection still runs on every row and is
sliced afterwards: its GEMV rounds differently at another row count, which
moved selections on the ratio-2 layers. The FP8 query projection quantizes
per row and is row-count invariant. Candidate-source outputs stay rank-local:
the later candidate layers read only the same rank's rows. Decode and non-SP
forwards keep the full-row path.

SPARK3_DS41_INDEXER_SPLIT_CHECK=1 additionally runs the full-row indexer
twice under SP and logs, per layer, how far the split's selections differ
from the first run beside how far the second run does: the radix top-k keeps
an arbitrary subset of the positions tied at its threshold, so full-row runs
differ too. Unwritten rows and changed candidate lists are warnings.
